{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head 56c7b8ed07f9692ab2b72574dedba4487425945e on planner/live-level-20261110, all four artifacts, for request bf1992ae under promise 8612669f and review request e5712915. The design holds. I accept the kernel exception of ruling 4b5e0ec2, so the level does not wait on that point. Three statements contradict another part of the design or reuse a contract word with a new meaning. An implementer of LV1 and LV2 would have to guess which one is meant.\n\nThe kernel review, which section 7 of `docs/kernel.md` asks for:\n\n- The argument holds. A live act writes no entry, so no recorded entry depends on the code that runs it. That code passes the replacement test of section 2 and belongs to the runtime host as a platform service.\n- Two parts stay kernel, and they are small. The validator must admit `live` version 1 and refuse the durable forms, because kernel code validates a pinned definition (7.2). The fold must refuse a third entry, because the runtime and replay must agree that such a journal is wrong.\n- The exception is narrow. Ruling 4b5e0ec2 rests on the owner's decision in order fc7576f8, covers only the first version of section 7, and closes no K item. The level adds no scope kind, platform name, capability, entry kind or field type, so K1 and K4 do not grow. The kernel.md text in sections 7 and 7.1 says the same, in the form of the sequenced precedent 08c5ff25.\n- The first version is a sound cut. It leaves out session-owned items, messaging, content addresses, cross-scope live references, host views, multi-frame snapshots and a WebSocket, and the table of section 7 states what the level does without each.\n\nRequired changes:\n\n1. The sequenced note's section 3 table still contradicts this note. Its Live row (line 100) reads \"What survives: Nothing\" and \"Cited as a fact: No\". This note's section 2 says that the genesis entry and the creator's confirmation survive, and that a fact may cite the two creation entries. Section 9 says the head changes \"Sequenced note, sections 2 and 3\". The head changes the section 2 row and the prose under the section 3 table, not the row. Correct the row.\n2. \"Revision\" has two definitions. Section 2 (line 72) says it is \"the count of inputs applied in the generation\". Section 3.3 says it starts at the sequence of the journal's head and that each applied input adds one. The second is the one that keeps live item IDs clear of the genesis items. Use it in section 2.\n3. \"Revision\" already names something else in the same turn. `Item.revision` (`packages/contract/src/read.ts:58`) is a per-item counter, and `revision-moved` refers to it. Section 3.3 judges \"the `expected` revisions\", which are item revisions, and two paragraphs later uses \"the revision\" for the scope's counter. `LiveAnswer` and every live read then carry a `revision` that is not an item's. Give the scope counter its own name, or define both senses side by side and name the field so that a reader of `LiveAnswer` cannot take it for an item revision.\n\nSmaller corrections, which the next head can take with the three above:\n\n- Section 3.5 says that the sequence document of the identifiers note \"describes a live scope as a sequence of two entries\". The identifiers note (5.3, section 8) says nothing of live scopes. Section 5 of this note gives the same fact as a property of the design. Word 3.5 the same way.\n- Section 6 omits one withdrawal. The volatile note's 16.5 witness reads \"A field of type `fact` that names a live scope is refused\" (line 2034). Section 3.8 now lets a `fact` name either creation entry. List it with the other withdrawals.\n- Section 8 says that \"the cutover family is the first\" family to install a live scope. The cutover family rulings 68356c6c do not mention a live scope. Cite the source or drop the clause.\n- `touched` in `applied` (line 169) has no definition. No contract type has the field.\n- The answer `applied` shares its word with the send-result clause `applied` (`read.ts:112`, `derive/src/delivery.ts:289`). The two live in different types, so this needs no rename. A sentence in 3.4 that says so would spare the confusion when a live scope's own creation confirms with an `applied` clause.\n- O1 proposes the refusal `scope-full` for a byte bound in memory. `Answer`'s comment ties `scope-full` to the exhausted whole-scope budget (`result.ts:78`). Say that the meaning widens, or name the bound's refusal.\n- Section 3.1 says that `lane` is the kind of every scope with a digest-pinned definition. The platform mints such seeds as `lane` (`platform/src/application.ts:112`), but the `create` validator accepts any kind with a digest definition (`derive/src/validate/sends.ts:194`). Say that the root sends a `lane` seed, or let LV1 refuse a live definition under any other kind.\n- Section 4.2 does not say which refusal codes the fold and the validator use for the new refusals. If LV1 needs a new code in `contract`, kernel.md section 7 counts it. Name the existing codes, or tell LV1 to say so.\n- Line 28 pins source facts at main 73e30699. The head's base is 7177b87b. The two differ only in the mcp README and test support, so every fact holds. Update the pin.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Scope. Main 7177b87b is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the planner's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the four paths that its four artifacts cover, all Markdown. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The commit-message check passes: 2 checked, 0 flagged, including the merge from origin/main. `git diff --check` is clean. On my own extracted copy, `check-doc-links.mjs` checks 1,228 local links and finds none broken. `check-section-citations.mjs` reports two unresolved references, both in `spikes/typed-surface/lib.ts`, which the head does not touch.\n- The citations. A separate read at main checked 9 source citations, 8 existence claims and 16 section references. Each holds: `Streams` with `STREAMS = 64`, `confirmation` in `delivery.ts`, `admitChildCreation`, `DurabilityLevel`, `validateFormat1`, `Answer`, `Read`, `ScopeApi`, the app-only `view_read`, `dap act`, the corpus row and the replay test that refuse `live` today, and kernel promises 1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10 and 11 as quoted. The section 6 table matches 16.1 to 16.6 and L1 to L7. `SequenceRef`, `/v1/sequences`, `did:seq` and the genesis-seed `cause` rule match the identifiers note. Decisions 1 to 10 are numbered without a gap.\n- The plan. LV1 to LV5 follow the dependency order, keep one gate each, and agree with the parallel-lanes sentence. Section 9 matches the edits that the head makes to kernel.md and the volatile note.\n\nArchitecture: sound, apart from the three corrections. A live scope is an ordinary `lane` with two entries, so references, identifiers head (c+d) and the directory need no change. Each generation starts from the fold of the genesis, and the live turn uses the judges of `derive`, so a live run and a recorded run of one definition reach the same state.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. A live act is an ordinary signed intent under a membership grant, so the level adds no signing domain and no path around authorization. The validator refuses sends, capabilities, holds and `fact` operands in a live definition, the package check refuses a send to a live scope, and no recorded guard reads live state, so live state cannot leak into the record. A watch checks the session before each send, and streams stay at 64 for each scope. A repeat after a reset can apply again, and section 3.3 tells an application that cannot accept that to use a recorded scope. LV2's witness should include that case.\n\nSimplification: no further change. The kernel part is two refusals and one admission, and everything else is replaceable host code.\n\nThis verdict authorizes no landing. The kernel exception stands under ruling 4b5e0ec2, and a new head with the three required changes can come back for review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:04af3ec1f3027ae102232dd59e6f9eca86a92e52","binding":"assigned","head":"56c7b8ed07f9692ab2b72574dedba4487425945e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:67719b9f99faaca9797bdbee928f6fa28dd83c43\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8612669f54d4ccccf03a3ab1ab46016296a892e8\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d152ada14212a7e132778dc2f10459ab7bf9d693","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}